{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "moves.operating_mode",
    "description": "Template library for the operating-mode-distribution generator family. Ports the arithmetic of `StartOperatingModeDistributionGenerator.java` steps 100-400 and `RatesOperatingModeDistributionGenerator.java` steps 200 and 210, via crates/moves-calculators/src/generators/start_operating_mode_distribution.rs and .../rates_op_mode_distribution.rs. Specified in docs/operating-mode-distribution.md.\n\nWHAT THE FAMILY SHARES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. The working hypothesis this rung began from was that the five operating-mode-distribution generators share one spine and differ at the edges. Measured against the pinned MOVES source, they do not: `RatesOperatingModeDistributionGenerator` is a cross-product of hotelling op modes with the run's hour-days, `StartOperatingModeDistributionGenerator` is a soak-time histogram over sample vehicle trips, `AverageSpeedOperatingModeDistributionGenerator` is a drive-schedule bracketing, and `Link`/`MesoscaleLookup` are project- and mesoscale-domain rewrites of the second-by-second VSP pipeline. Not one arithmetic expression is common to any two of them. What IS common -- and what this file therefore holds -- is the SHAPE OF THE ROW they all write into `RatesOpModeDistribution`: road type 1, average-speed bin 0, bin speed 0, and, for the degenerate distributions, a fraction of exactly 1. docs/esm-conventions.md section 39.1 records the measurement that refuted the hypothesis; this file is what survived it.\n\nSO THE SPINE IS THE OUTPUT ROW, NOT THE ARITHMETIC, and it is worth being precise about the sharing that justifies each constant rather than asserting a family resemblance. `off_network_road_type` is written by four separate `INSERT` statements (Rates step 200 SQL 1 and SQL 2, Rates step 210 SQL 1 and SQL 2) plus two of `StartOperatingModeDistributionGenerator`'s step-400 statements; `off_network_avg_speed_bin` by the same six; `degenerate_op_mode_fraction` by five of them; `extended_idle_op_mode` by three (once as the mode step 200 SQL 2 guarantees, twice as the mode step 210 excludes); `hotelling_source_type` by four. A literal written in six places is the repetition docs/esm-conventions.md section 6 exists to prevent, whatever the modules around it look like.\n\nTHE ARITHMETIC IS ALL `StartOperatingModeDistributionGenerator`'S, and there is exactly one piece of it: `op_mode_fraction`. `soak_time` is a subtraction, `soak_band_contains` is a predicate, `hour_day_id` is a key composition. The one place a number is computed rather than selected is `COUNT(opModeID)/starts`, and that division is not the IEEE ratio -- see below.\n\nTHE QUOTIENT IS A FOUR-DECIMAL DECIMAL, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Both operands of `COUNT(opModeID)/starts` are MariaDB `BIGINT`, and `/` between exact-value operands yields DECIMAL with the dividend's scale plus `div_precision_increment`, which MOVES leaves at its default of 4. moves.rs's port deferred the question -- it returns the exact f64 ratio and its module doc calls the four-place rounding 'still untested' -- and the corpus settles it. Over the 124 `StartOpModeDistribution` rows the nine populated snapshots carry, the four-decimal quotient reproduces every single value BIT-EXACTLY -- 124 of 124, worst absolute error 0.0 -- and the exact ratio reproduces only the 15 whose value happens to terminate at four decimals, missing 109 at a worst absolute error of 4.7619e-05 and a worst relative error of 5.767e-03. 107 of the 124 land outside tolerance.toml's 2e-5 per-cell gate, so the wrong candidate fails loudly rather than hiding. docs/esm-conventions.md section 39.2 is the rule and docs/operating-mode-distribution.md section 7.2 has the measurement. It is NOT a `docs/findings/` entry: that directory holds EarthSciAST format defects with executable repros, and this is a fact about MOVES. This is the mirror image of section 35.3's meteorology result and the reason the rule there is stated as 'evaluate both candidates', not 'MOVES uses exact arithmetic'.\n\n`decimal_quotient` TAKES ITS SCALE AS A PARAMETER, because the family needs two of them and the reason is one rule rather than two facts. MariaDB gives `a / b` between exact-value operands a scale of the DIVIDEND's scale plus `div_precision_increment`. `StartOperatingModeDistributionGenerator` divides `COUNT(opModeID)` -- a `BIGINT`, scale 0 -- so it gets 0+4 = FOUR decimals. `LinkOperatingModeDistributionGenerator` writes `secondCount * 1.0 / secondTotal`, and the `* 1.0` makes the dividend a DECIMAL of scale 1, so it gets 1+4 = FIVE. One rule, one `* 1.0` apart, one digit apart; `op_mode_fraction` and `link_op_mode_fraction` bind the two scales and share the rounding. Both are measured against the corpus -- 124 of 124 `StartOpModeDistribution` cells at four decimals, 80 of 80 bracketing `OpModeDistribution` cells at five -- and in both cases the neighbouring scale misses nearly every cell (109 of 124 there, 76 of 80 here). docs/link-operating-mode-distribution.md section 2.4.\n\n`decimal_quotient` is written as `floor(x * scale + 1/2) / scale` rather than as a rounding intrinsic because the format has `floor` and no `round`, and because writing the scale as a named constant keeps `div_precision_increment` -- the MariaDB server variable the behaviour actually depends on -- legible in the document. MariaDB rounds DECIMAL half AWAY FROM ZERO; both operands here are `COUNT()` results and so non-negative, which makes half-away-from-zero and half-up the same function on this domain. lib/population.esm's `pop_file_rounding` is the same shape at scale 10 and is deliberately NOT reused: it is NONROAD's population file format, a different fact that happens to round the same way, and coupling them would make one of the two lie the day either changes.\n\nTHE SOAK BAND IS SQL THREE-VALUED LOGIC AND `in_half_open_range` IS NOT IT. lib/drive_cycle.esm already carries a half-open interval test, and reusing it here would be wrong in exactly one case, which is the case the corpus checks. The Java condition is `(minSoakTime <= soakTime OR (minSoakTime IS NULL AND maxSoakTime IS NOT NULL)) AND (maxSoakTime > soakTime OR (maxSoakTime IS NULL AND minSoakTime IS NOT NULL))`. When BOTH bounds are absent each clause is `NULL OR FALSE`, i.e. UNKNOWN, and `UNKNOWN AND UNKNOWN` is not TRUE -- so a bandless operating mode matches NOTHING. `in_half_open_range` returns 1 there, because an absent bound is a bound that cannot exclude. The corpus makes the difference load-bearing: 52 of the `OperatingMode` table's 60 rows carry no soak band at all, operating mode 100 ('Starting (Used for all starts)') among them, and MOVES emits no `StartOpModeDistribution` row for any of them. Under `in_half_open_range` every one of the 262,442 sample-vehicle starts the corpus classifies would land in all 52.\n\n`sql_bound_clause` is the two-clause symmetry factored out rather than written twice. The Java's lower and upper clauses are mirror images -- each says 'if MY bound is present, compare against it; otherwise I am satisfied exactly when the OTHER bound is present' -- so there is one template applied twice with the roles swapped, and `soak_band_contains` is their conjunction. `min` is the conjunction of two 0/1 indicators; `truth` is what turns a comparison into one, and it exists because docs/esm-conventions.md section 11 warns that a parameter which failed to ingest arrives as its `default` rather than as a null, so presence is carried as its own indicator column and never inferred from a value.\n\n`insert_ignore_union` is `max`, and it is a template rather than a bare `max` because the reason it is sound is not local. MOVES writes `RatesOpModeDistribution` with `INSERT IGNORE`, so the second statement of a step silently loses to the first on a primary-key collision. That would matter if the two statements could disagree, and here they cannot: every row either step emits carries `opModeFraction = 1` and `avgBinSpeed = 0`, so a collision is always between value-identical rows and the union is order-independent. Written as `max` of two presence indicators, the expression says the rows are a SET; the name says why that is allowed to stand in for a sequence of statements.",
    "authors": ["EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"],
    "tags": ["moves", "onroad", "operating-mode", "generator", "template-library"]
  },
  "expression_templates": {
    "hotelling_source_type": { "params": [], "body": 62 },
    "off_network_road_type": { "params": [], "body": 1 },
    "off_network_avg_speed_bin": { "params": [], "body": 0 },
    "off_network_avg_bin_speed": { "params": [], "body": 0.0 },
    "degenerate_op_mode_fraction": { "params": [], "body": 1.0 },
    "extended_idle_op_mode": { "params": [], "body": 200 },
    "all_starts_op_mode": { "params": [], "body": 100 },
    "all_starts_pol_process": { "params": [], "body": 602 },
    "hour_day_radix": { "params": [], "body": 10 },
    "div_precision_increment": { "params": [], "body": 4 },
    "decimal_division_scale": { "params": [], "body": 10000.0 },
    "scaled_dividend_division_scale": { "params": [], "body": 100000.0 },

    "truth": {
      "params": ["condition"],
      "body": { "op": "ifelse", "args": ["condition", 1.0, 0.0] }
    },

    "hour_day_id": {
      "params": ["hour_id", "day_id"],
      "body": {
        "op": "+",
        "args": [
          { "op": "*", "args": [
            "hour_id",
            { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "hour_day_radix", "bindings": {} }] },
          "day_id"
        ]
      }
    },

    "soak_time": {
      "params": ["key_on_time", "prior_key_off_time"],
      "body": { "op": "-", "args": ["key_on_time", "prior_key_off_time"] }
    },

    "sql_bound_clause": {
      "params": ["this_bound_present", "other_bound_present", "comparison_holds"],
      "body": {
        "op": "ifelse",
        "args": [
          { "op": ">", "args": ["this_bound_present", 0.0] },
          "comparison_holds",
          { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "truth",
            "bindings": { "condition": { "op": ">", "args": ["other_bound_present", 0.0] } } }
        ]
      }
    },

    "soak_band_contains": {
      "params": ["soak_time", "min_soak_time", "max_soak_time",
                 "has_min_soak_time", "has_max_soak_time"],
      "body": {
        "op": "min",
        "args": [
          { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "sql_bound_clause",
            "bindings": {
              "this_bound_present": "has_min_soak_time",
              "other_bound_present": "has_max_soak_time",
              "comparison_holds": {
                "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "truth",
                "bindings": { "condition": { "op": "<=", "args": ["min_soak_time", "soak_time"] } } }
            } },
          { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "sql_bound_clause",
            "bindings": {
              "this_bound_present": "has_max_soak_time",
              "other_bound_present": "has_min_soak_time",
              "comparison_holds": {
                "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "truth",
                "bindings": { "condition": { "op": ">", "args": ["max_soak_time", "soak_time"] } } }
            } }
        ]
      }
    },

    "decimal_quotient": {
      "params": ["numerator", "denominator", "scale"],
      "body": {
        "op": "/",
        "args": [
          { "op": "floor", "args": [
            { "op": "+", "args": [
              { "op": "*", "args": [
                { "op": "/", "args": ["numerator", "denominator"] },
                "scale"] },
              0.5] }] },
          "scale"
        ]
      }
    },

    "op_mode_fraction": {
      "params": ["op_mode_count", "starts"],
      "body": {
        "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "decimal_quotient",
        "bindings": { "numerator": "op_mode_count", "denominator": "starts",
                      "scale": { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [],
                                 "name": "decimal_division_scale", "bindings": {} } }
      }
    },

    "link_op_mode_fraction": {
      "params": ["second_count", "second_total"],
      "body": {
        "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "decimal_quotient",
        "bindings": { "numerator": "second_count", "denominator": "second_total",
                      "scale": { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [],
                                 "name": "scaled_dividend_division_scale", "bindings": {} } }
      }
    },

    "excludes_extended_idle": {
      "params": ["op_mode_id"],
      "body": {
        "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "truth",
        "bindings": { "condition": { "op": "!=", "args": ["op_mode_id",
          { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "extended_idle_op_mode", "bindings": {} }] } }
      }
    },

    "insert_ignore_union": {
      "params": ["first_statement", "second_statement"],
      "body": { "op": "max", "args": ["first_statement", "second_statement"] }
    }
  }
}
